# Kconfig settings for compiling and running the micro-controller code
# in a Linux process

if MACH_LINUX

config LINUX_SELECT
    bool
    default y
    select HAVE_GPIO
    select HAVE_GPIO_ADC
    select HAVE_GPIO_SPI
    select HAVE_GPIO_I2C
    select HAVE_GPIO_HARD_PWM

config LINUX_CANSERIAL
    bool "CAN bus (on a SocketCAN interface)"
    select CANSERIAL
    help
        Speak can-serial over a CAN interface the kernel already provides
        (linux/can.c), rather than over this process's own console. The
        interface is named on the command line (`-c`, default can0) and has to
        exist; its bit timing is the interface's own, set with `ip link` - a
        vcan interface has none.

        SERIAL stays on beside this: a linux process's console is
        linux/console.c, not the serial backend that symbol selects on other
        arches (generic/serial_irq.c is not built here), so there is no choice
        between the two carriers to make. The pty this target always opens
        stays as well, as the marker its `-I` path points at: with a CAN
        console the protocol runs over the socket, not the pty.

config BOARD_DIRECTORY
    string
    default "linux"

config LINUX_BRIDGE_SERIAL
    bool "Bridge mode: serve one tty as a downstream port"
    default n
    select BRIDGE_PORT_SERIAL
    select MUX_SERIAL
    help
        A linux process's console is one byte stream (linux/console.c), and a
        bridge build hands it more than one port. With this, the process also
        serves a downstream port of its own: a UART object the host creates
        with config_uart opens the tty the launcher named for it in the
        environment (KLIPPER_BRIDGE_PORT_0), at the rate that same command
        carried, and the router (src/bridge.c) writes and reads that port.

        Nothing is opened at init - which tty a port is on is a configuration
        fact - so a port with no tty named, or one that cannot be opened, fails
        that configuration explicitly instead of leaving a bridge that comes up
        without its port. One port is served (slot 0), and a port's RESET_PORT
        has no line to pulse on a tty, so it is dropped in silence.

        This is also a mux build (MUX_SERIAL): the port-level commands and the
        tunnel a flashing host opens travel the console's mux admin channel,
        and the routing bridge comes with the mux half (src/Kconfig's BRIDGE
        `default y if`). On by itself, the process is a mux device with no
        ports - its own klipper instance and nothing else.

config SERIAL
    default y

config CLOCK_FREQ
    int
    default 50000000

endif
